course: dictionary
lesson: 2789
title: Words for things pushed out of place
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words are built from parts you already know. The start dis- often means apart or away from, and location means a place. Put them together and dislocation means something has been moved away from its proper place.
explain: «The shoulder is a ball-and-socket joint and the most mobile joint in the body; its socket is shallow, so it moves freely but can pop out, which is called dislocation.» The clue is pop out. A shoulder that pops out of its socket has been pushed out of its normal place, and that is a dislocation.
explain: The same word can also describe people. A flood or a war can force whole families from their homes, and this serious disturbance is also called dislocation. Always check the words around it: are we talking about a body, or about people and their lives?
fact: Dislocation is a noun: a dislocation, dislocations; it can also be used as an uncountable noun, dislocation.
fact: In health, dislocation means a bone pushed out of its normal place at a joint, such as «a shoulder dislocation».
fact: A dislocation of this kind is treated at hospital.
fact: In history, geography and economics, dislocation means a serious disturbance where people or things are moved from where they should be, as in «social dislocation».
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence: «The shoulder was pushed out of its normal place» still makes sense, so you have understood it.
exercise: In «The shoulder is a ball-and-socket joint», what can dislocation mean for a shoulder? | A loud click when you stretch your arm | A strong muscle that never tires | A bone that has grown very long | *A bone pushed out of its normal place at a joint
exercise: After the flood, many families faced social dislocation. What does it mean here? | A new road built across the hills | A happy party for the whole street | A warm, dry summer | *A serious disturbance, with people moved from where they should be
exercise: What is the plural of the noun dislocation? | dislocated | dislocating | dislocates | *dislocations
test: Why can the shoulder pop out, according to «its socket is shallow, so it moves freely but can pop out»? | It has no bones at all | Its socket is very deep and tight | *Its socket is shallow, so it moves freely | It never moves from one place
test: Which phrase uses dislocation for people forced from their homes? | shoulder dislocation | *social dislocation | hospital bed | ball-and-socket joint
exam: In «a shoulder dislocation», what has happened to the shoulder? | *It has been pushed out of its normal place | It has been painted a new colour | It has grown bigger than before | It has been wrapped in a warm bandage
